    slug: 'indiana-post-derecho-tree-removal-price-gouging',
    description: `In the weeks after an August 11, 2026 derecho brought 99 mph wind gusts, widespread flooding, and power outages to more than 300,000 utility customers across Indiana, several tree-removal and storm-cleanup companies moved into the hardest-hit communities and charged desperate homeowners far more than the work was worth. Garrison McKinney Tree and Bridge Services is accused of charging an elderly Lake County woman $35,000 to remove two trees after initially quoting her $40,000, warning her that leaving the trees standing endangered neighbors and nearby park visitors. Storm Support Emergency Tree Removal is accused of operating without the license Indiana requires, failing to disclose estimates or rates up front, and charging thousands of dollars more than customers had been told to expect, with at least five identified victims found from northern Indiana to Indianapolis. Attorney General Todd Rokita's office said its outreach team visited more than 40 storm-affected communities and knocked on over 750 doors before filing suit on September 2, 2026 against Garrison McKinney Tree and Bridge Services, Storm Support Emergency Tree Removal, Shade Tree Service Co., and TimberCut Tree Experts, seeking injunctive relief, restitution, and civil penalties of up to $5,000 per documented violation. "When you're at your lowest emotionally, and you're weary, that's when the bad guys will be bad guys," Rokita said, calling the conduct "reprehensible" and vowing to "chase them out of the state." Homeowners hiring storm cleanup after a disaster are advised to get a written estimate before any work begins, to confirm a tree-removal company's Indiana license before signing anything, and to be especially wary of a contractor who shows up unsolicited in a damaged neighborhood and pressures an immediate decision.`,
