    slug: 'mississippi-corinth-medical-plaza-pharmacy-phantom-prescription-fraud',
    description: `Ricky Wayne Quinn and John Anthony "Andy" Null, co-owners of Medical Plaza Pharmacy in Corinth, Mississippi, ran a billing scheme between 2017 and 2021 in which the pharmacy knowingly submitted false and fraudulent claims to Medicare and Medicaid for prescription drugs that were never actually dispensed to patients. According to court records, the pair repeatedly billed the same high-reimbursement prescription products — including diabetic insulin pens, asthma inhalers, and psychotropic medications — multiple times against a single prescription, generating reimbursements far beyond what the pharmacy had actually filled. The U.S. Attorney's Office for the Northern District of Mississippi and the Mississippi Attorney General's Office, working with the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services Office of Inspector General, prosecuted the case as conspiracy to commit healthcare fraud; Null was sentenced on April 20, 2026, and Quinn was sentenced on June 29, 2026 to 30 months in federal prison plus three years of supervised release, with both men ordered to jointly pay $786,972.10 in restitution to the Mississippi Division of Medicaid. "We are sending a clear message that here in Mississippi, we are committed to fighting waste, fraud and corruption," Attorney General Lynn Fitch said in announcing the sentencing. Medicare and Medicaid beneficiaries can protect themselves and the programs by reviewing every Explanation of Benefits or Medicare Summary Notice they receive for prescriptions or services they don't recognize, keeping a personal log of medications actually picked up, and reporting suspected billing for drugs or services never received to the Mississippi Attorney General's Medicaid Fraud Control Unit or the HHS-OIG hotline.`,
