    slug: 'iowa-temu-shop-like-a-billionaire-consumer-fraud-lawsuit',
    description: `Iowa Attorney General Brenna Bird filed suit against Temu — the online marketplace operated by PDD Holdings (formerly Pinduoduo) and its U.S. entity Whaleco Inc. — in Polk County District Court on July 1, 2026, alleging the platform's "shop like a billionaire" marketing promise is built on a pattern of deception that violates the Iowa Consumer Fraud Act. The lawsuit alleges Temu lures Iowa shoppers with "sign-up scams" and misrepresents the quality of merchandise and the size of advertised discounts against inflated reference prices, while separately harvesting far more personal data than a shopping app needs to function — including precise location data, records of other apps installed on a user's phone, device identifiers, and Wi-Fi network information — data the complaint says could be accessed by the Chinese Communist Party under Chinese national security law. The suit also accuses Temu of trampling on Iowa intellectual property, including unauthorized use of University of Iowa Hawkeyes branding and the IOWA Wave design, whose legitimate sales fund pediatric cancer and illness research at University of Iowa Health Care Stead Family Children's Hospital. Iowa joins Texas, Oklahoma, Kentucky, Nebraska, and Arkansas in bringing similar consumer-protection actions against Temu, and the suit seeks an injunction ordering Temu to stop the unlawful conduct along with consumer restitution and civil penalties. Shoppers using Temu or similar low-cost overseas marketplace apps should review what data an app is permitted to access in their phone's privacy settings, compare a "sale" price against the item's actual price history on an independent site before buying, and be skeptical of promotional emails, texts, or in-app prompts pushing a "free gift" or spin-the-wheel offer that requires providing more personal information or recruiting other users to sign up.`,
